---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-shift-work`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D for Shift Workers and Indoor Workers',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Shift Work', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D for Shift Workers and Indoor Workers: Deficiency, Dosing, Circadian Angle"
  description="Night shifts, office jobs, healthcare, mining — all associate with vitamin D deficiency. Dosing guidance for people whose schedule fights UVB exposure."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-shift-work", label: "Vitamin D & Shift Work" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D for Shift Workers and Indoor Workers</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Any job that keeps you indoors during UVB hours — night shifts, office work, healthcare
      clinical shifts, mining, professional driving, factory work — sets you up for
      vitamin D deficiency. Studies of nurses, night-shift factory workers, and coal
      miners consistently find 40–70% of workers deficient regardless of season.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Why the day/night schedule matters</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      UVB reaches the ground in usable intensity only when the sun is more than about 35°
      above the horizon — roughly 10 AM to 2 PM at temperate latitudes. Sleep during those
      hours is more than an inconvenience; it removes the only window in which your skin
      can synthesise vitamin D. Weekend catch-up sun exposure helps but doesn't fully
      compensate over a 5-day/2-day pattern.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Occupational deficiency data</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Night-shift healthcare workers</strong> — Alefishat & Abu Farha (2016) found 76% of Jordanian night-shift nurses deficient vs 43% of day-shift.</li>
      <li><strong>Coal miners</strong> — Turkish study (Elmali 2018) found 90%+ deficient, with the underground years correlating with lower 25(OH)D.</li>
      <li><strong>Office workers</strong> — European studies find winter deficiency in 50%+; commute-in-the-dark and lunch-at-the-desk are enough to depress synthesis year-round.</li>
      <li><strong>Submariners, offshore oil workers</strong> — closed environments produce near-complete deficiency within weeks unless supplemented.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Consequences beyond bones</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Shift work is independently associated with cardiovascular disease, metabolic
      syndrome, and certain cancers — through disrupted circadian biology. Vitamin D
      deficiency compounds several of those pathways. Correcting deficiency doesn't reverse
      circadian misalignment, but it removes one accelerator.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dosing guide for indoor / shift schedules</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Test 25(OH)D — assume deficiency probable unless proven otherwise.</li>
      <li>Standard adult supplementation: 2,000 IU/day cholecalciferol year-round.</li>
      <li>If deficient (&lt; 20 ng/mL): 4,000 IU/day for 8–12 weeks, re-test, then 2,000 IU/day maintenance.</li>
      <li>Take with a fat-containing meal — for shift workers this may be your "dinner" at an unconventional hour.</li>
      <li>Sun exposure on days off helps but is not sufficient — plan on year-round supplementation.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Circadian vs vitamin D — separate problems, separate fixes</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Bright light exposure timed to your desired wake period entrains the circadian
      pacemaker. That's what SAD-style light boxes do at 10,000 lux — no vitamin D
      involved. For shift workers, the two interventions are complementary but distinct:
      light for circadian alignment, vitamin D for the endocrine deficiency.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If shift work is causing severe fatigue,
      cardiovascular symptoms, or mental health decline, the fix isn't only a vitamin —
      talk to an occupational health physician about schedule modification.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-shift-work" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
